<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zalesye</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zalesye ('the land beyond the woods') is the historical name of the north-eastern Rus' lands between the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and the Oka, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostov">Rostov</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a> and Vladimir, which from the viewpoint of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> lay beyond the great forests. The region formed the core of Vladimir-Suzdal and later of Muscovy, and the name survives in towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereslavl-zalessky">Pereslavl-Zalessky</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zalesye.jpg" alt="Zalesye" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: VadimV · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%90%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%BA%D1%81%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B4%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B0_%D0%B3%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B0.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
